nd AC, which are the axes. The radius is r, so p = r and q = r, so P is (r, 0) and Q is (0, r).\n\nThe circumcircle O has center at (c/2, b/2) and radius R = sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2.\n\nThe distance between the centers of O and O\u2081 is sqrt( (c/2 - r)^2 + (b/2 - r)^2 ) = R \u00b1 r. Since O\u2081 is inside O, it's R - r.\n\nTherefore:\n\nsqrt( (c/2 - r)^2 + (b/2 - r)^2 ) = R - r = sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2 - r\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\n(c/2 - r)^2 + (b/2 - r)^2 = (sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/2 - r)^2\n\nExpand left side:\n\nc\u00b2/4 - c r + r\u00b2 + b\u00b2/4 - b r + r\u00b2 = (c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 - r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + r\u00b2\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 - (c + b)r + 2r\u00b2\n\nRight side:\n\n(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 - r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + r\u00b2\n\nSet equal:\n\n(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 - (c + b)r + 2r\u00b2 = (c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 - r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + r\u00b2\n\nSubtract (c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/4 from both sides:\n\n- (c + b)r + 2r\u00b2 = - r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + r\u00b2\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n- (c + b)r + 2r\u00b2 + r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) - r\u00b2 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n- (c + b)r + r\u00b2 + r sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) = 0\n\nFactor out r:\n\nr [ - (c + b) + r + sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) ] = 0\n\nSince r \u2260 0, we have:\n\n- (c + b) + r + sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) = 0\n\nSolve for r:\n\nr = (c + b) - sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, the radius r is r = (c + b) - sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nThe midpoint M of PQ is (r/2, r/2) = [ (c + b - sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2))/2, (c + b - sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2))/2 ]\n\nNow, compute the inradius of triangle ABC. The inradius r_in = \u0394/s, where \u0394 is the area and s is the semiperimeter.\n\n\u0394 = (base * height)/2 = (c * b)/2\n\ns = (a + b + c)/2, where a = sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nThus, r_in = (c b / 2) / ( (sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + b + c)/2 ) = (c b ) / (sqrt(c\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + b + c )\n\nCompare this to the coordinates of M: each coordinate is (c + b