\u03b8 + 3 sin \u03b8)^2 = 9 (m\u00b2 + 1)\n\nExpanding left side:\n\n16 m\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 24 m cos \u03b8 sin \u03b8 + 9 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = 9 m\u00b2 + 9\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n16 m\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 24 m cos \u03b8 sin \u03b8 + 9 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 9 m\u00b2 - 9 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n(16 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - 9) m\u00b2 + 24 cos \u03b8 sin \u03b8 m + (9 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 9) = 0\n\nFactor out:\n\n(16 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - 9) m\u00b2 + 24 cos \u03b8 sin \u03b8 m + 9 (sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 1) = 0\n\nNote that sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 1 = -cos\u00b2 \u03b8, so:\n\n(16 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - 9) m\u00b2 + 24 cos \u03b8 sin \u03b8 m - 9 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in m. Solving for m would give the slopes of the two tangents from P to the circle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe parametrize the points of contact. Let me try a different approach. For a circle centered at the origin, the points of tangency from an external point P can be found using the parametric equations. If I consider the line OP (from origin to P), the points A and B lie on the circle such that PA and PB are tangent to the circle. The angle between OP and the tangent lines can be found using trigonometry.\n\nThe distance from P to the center O is sqrt( (4 cos \u03b8)^2 + (3 sin \u03b8)^2 ) = sqrt(16 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 9 sin\u00b2 \u03b8). The radius of the circle is 3, so the angle between OP and the tangent lines is given by sin \u03c6 = r / |OP|, where \u03c6 is the angle between OP and the tangent.\n\nTherefore, sin \u03c6 = 3 / sqrt(16 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 9 sin\u00b2 \u03b8). Then, the direction from P to A and B can be found by rotating OP by \u00b1\u03c6. But this might be a bit complex. Alternatively, using coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, recall that the points A and B lie on the circle, and PA is tangent to the circle. Therefore, OA is perpendicular to PA. Similarly, OB is perpendicular to PB.\n\nTherefore, vectors OA and PA are perpendicular. So, OA \u00b7 PA = 0.\n\nLet A = (x, y