ust satisfy proportion relationship)}\n\\]\n\n5. Solving for condition:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{m}}< 1\n\\Rightarrow \\frac{5}{m} < 1 \n\\Rightarrow 5 < m \\Rightarrow m > 5\n\\]\nThe correct choice should satisfy this condition.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(D)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( AE \\) and \\( CD \\) be the angle bisectors of the isosceles triangle \\( ABC \\) (\\( AB = BC \\)). Prove that \\( \\angle BED = 2 \\angle AED \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that in an isosceles triangle ABC where AB equals BC, the angle bisectors AE and CD make angle BED twice angle AED. Hmm, let me start by drawing a diagram to visualize the problem.\n\nFirst, since ABC is isosceles with AB = BC, that means the triangle is actually ABC with AB = BC, so the base is AC, and the two equal sides are AB and BC. Wait, but in a typical isosceles triangle, the equal sides are adjacent. If AB = BC, then the triangle has vertex at B, and the base is AC. So the base angles at A and C would be equal. Let me confirm that.\n\nIn triangle ABC, AB = BC, so vertex B is the apex, and the base is AC. Therefore, angles at A and C are equal. So angle at A (angle BAC) equals angle at C (angle BCA). That makes sense. Now, AE and CD are the angle bisectors. Let me figure out where points E and D are located.\n\nAngle bisector AE: since it's the bisector of angle at A, so AE splits angle BAC into two equal parts. Similarly, CD is the angle bisector of angle BCA, so it splits angle BCA into two equal parts. Since angles at A and C are equal, their bisectors will split them into equal smaller angles.\n\nNow, points D and E must lie on the sides of the triangle. Let me clarify the notation. In triangle ABC, AE is the angle bisector from A, so E must be a point on BC. Similarly, CD is the angle bisector from C, so D must be a point on AB. Wait, but in angle bisector terminology, the angle bise