rrightarrow{DC}\\), and \\(\\overrightarrow{BC}\\) must be parallel to \\(\\overrightarrow{DA}\\).\n9. Therefore, since \\( \\overrightarrow{AB} \\parallel \\overrightarrow{DC} \\) and \\( \\overrightarrow{BC} \\parallel \\overrightarrow{DA} \\), we conclude that \\(ABCD\\) is a parallelogram.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ABCD\\text{ is a parallelogram}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( X \\) be a random variable with a characteristic function \\( \\varphi = \\varphi(t) \\) such that \\( \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} |\\varphi(t)| \\, dt < \\infty \\). Using the fact that \\( \\psi(t) = (1 - |t|)^{+} \\) is the characteristic function of a distribution with the density\n\n\\[ g(x) = \\frac{1 - \\cos x}{\\pi x^2}, \\quad x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\]\n\nprove that \\( X \\) has a bounded continuous density given by the formula\n\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{1}{2 \\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{-i t x} \\varphi(t) \\, dt, \\quad x \\in \\mathbb{R}. \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the random variable X has a bounded continuous density given by that integral formula. The problem mentions using the characteristic function \u03c8(t) = (1 - |t|)\u207a, which corresponds to the density g(x) = (1 - cos x)/(\u03c0x\u00b2). Hmm, let me start by recalling some properties of characteristic functions and densities.\n\nFirst, I know that if a characteristic function \u03c6(t) is absolutely integrable, i.e., \u222b|\u03c6(t)| dt < \u221e, then the corresponding distribution has a continuous density given by the inverse Fourier transform of \u03c6(t). The formula given for f(x) is exactly that inverse Fourier transform. So maybe this problem is about showing that under the integrability condition on \u03c6, the density exists and is given by that formula. But the problem specifically mentions using \u03c8(t) and its density g(x), so I need to figure out how they relate here.\n\nLet me write down what I kn