vide the sequence and analyze the first few terms):**\n   We aim to find the maximum value of \\( a_6 \\), which can be achieved by maximizing \\( a_6 = a_1 + d_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 + d_5 \\).\n\n5. **Step 5 (Use arithmetic mean to bound the sum of initial differences):**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d_{1} + d_{2} + \\ldots + d_{5}}{5} \\leq \\frac{d_{6} + d_{7} + \\ldots + d_{504}}{499} = \\frac{2016 - (d_{1} + d_{2} + \\ldots + d_{5})}{499}\n   \\]\n   Multiplying both sides by 5:\n   \\[\n   d_{1} + d_{2} + \\ldots + d_{5} \\leq \\frac{5 \\cdot 2016}{499 + 5} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 2016}{504} = 20\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   \\[\n   a_6 = a_1 + d_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 + d_5 \\leq 1 + 20 = 21\n   \\]\n   Thus, the maximum value of \\( a_6 \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{21}\n   \\]\n\n#### Part (2)\n1. **Step 1 (Assume contradiction and establish properties):**\n   Suppose there exists some \\( k \\in \\mathbb{N}^* \\) such that \\( a_k < a_{k+1} \\). Given:\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1} \\leq \\frac{a_{n} + a_{n+2}}{2}\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1} \\leq a_{k} - a_{k+1} + a_{k+2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a_{k+1} > a_k \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1} < a_{k+2}\n   \\]\n   By induction, \\( a_n \\) is strictly increasing, which contradicts \\( S_n \\leq 1 \\) for large \\( n \\).\n\n2. **Step 2 (Series sum properties and rearrangement):**\n   Given the contradiction, \\( a_n \\geq a_{n+1} \\). Define:\n   \\[\n   b_k = a_k - a_{k+1}\n   \\]\n   \\( b_k \\) is positive and non-increasing, i.e., \\( b_k \\geq b_{k+1} \\geq 0 \\).\n\n3. **Step 3 (Sum of sequence and bounding inequalities):**\n   From the given bound \\( S_n \\leq 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   S_n = a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_n \\leq 1\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   1 \\geq a_1 + \\ldots + a_n = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} kb_k = b_1 + 2b_2 + \\ldots + nb_n\n   \\]\n   The inequality:\n   \\[\n   b_1 + 2b_2 + \\ldots + nb_n \\geq \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} b_n\n   \\]\n\n4. **Step 4 (Finalizing the upper bound for differences):**\n   Therefore:\n 