r x\u2081 -2x\u2082 \u22640.\n\nExpress this in terms of the sum and product.\n\nWe know that x\u2081 +x\u2082 = -b/a and x\u2081x\u2082 =c/a.\n\nLet\u2019s denote S = x\u2081 +x\u2082 = -b/a and P =x\u2081x\u2082 =c/a.\n\nWe need to express x\u2081 -2x\u2082 \u22640 in terms of S and P.\n\nLet\u2019s compute x\u2081 -2x\u2082:\n\nx\u2081 -2x\u2082 = (x\u2081 +x\u2082) -3x\u2082 = S -3x\u2082 \u22640 => S \u22643x\u2082.\n\nBut x\u2082 is the larger root. Alternatively, we can write x\u2081 = S -x\u2082. Then x\u2081 -2x\u2082 = (S -x\u2082) -2x\u2082 = S -3x\u2082 \u22640 => S \u22643x\u2082.\n\nBut x\u2082 is the larger root, so x\u2082 = [ -b + sqrt(D) ]/(2a). But this might get complicated.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the quadratic formula. Since x\u2081 and x\u2082 are roots:\n\nx\u2081 = [-b - sqrt(D)]/(2a)\n\nx\u2082 = [-b + sqrt(D)]/(2a)\n\nThen, x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082:\n\n[-b - sqrt(D)]/(2a) \u22642*[-b + sqrt(D)]/(2a)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2a. Since a >0, the inequality direction remains the same.\n\nSo:\n\n(-b - sqrt(D)) \u22642*(-b + sqrt(D))\n\nSimplify:\n\n-b - sqrt(D) \u2264 -2b +2sqrt(D)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n(-b - sqrt(D)) +2b -2sqrt(D) \u22640\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nb -3sqrt(D) \u22640\n\nThus:\n\nb \u22643sqrt(D)\n\nBut D is (k -13)^2, so sqrt(D)=|k -13|. Since k is in [6,11], k -13 is negative, so sqrt(D)=13 -k.\n\nTherefore, sqrt(D)=13 -k.\n\nSo, substituting back into the inequality:\n\nb \u22643*(13 -k)\n\nBut b=3k -7.\n\nTherefore:\n\n3k -7 \u22643*(13 -k)\n\nSolve for k:\n\n3k -7 \u226439 -3k\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n3k +3k -7 -39 \u22640\n\n6k -46 \u22640\n\n6k \u226446\n\nk \u226446/6 \u22487.6667.\n\nTherefore, the condition x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082 translates to k \u226446/6\u22487.6667.\n\nBut k is in [6,11]. Therefore, the values of k in [6, 46/6]\u2248[6,7.6667] satisfy the condition.\n\nTherefore, the length of the interval where k satisfies the condition is 46/6 -6 = (46/6 -36/6)=10/6=5/3\u22481.6667.\n\nBut the total interval is 11 -6=5.\n\nTherefore, the probability is (5/3)/5=1/3\u22480.3333.\n\nBut wait, let's verify this conclusion step by step because this seems straightforward, but let's check with an example.\n\nTake k=7, which is in [6,46/6\u22487.6667]. Let's compute the quadratic equation:\n\na=7\u00b2 -2*7 -15=49 -14 -15=20\n\nb=3*7 -7=21 -7=14\n\nc=2\n\nEquation:20x\u00b2 +14x +2=0\n\nCompute the roots:\n\nx = [-14 \u00b1sqrt(14\u00b2 -4*20*2)]/(2*20)=[-14 \u00b1sqrt(196 -160)]/40=[-14 \u00b1sqrt(36)]/40=[-14 \u00b16]/40\n\nThus, x\u2081=(-14 -6)/40= -20/40=-0.5, x\u2082=(-14 +6)/40=(-8)/40=-0.2\n\nSo, x\u2081=-0.5, x\u2082=-0.2\n\nCheck x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082: -0.5 \u22642*(-0.2)= -0.4. Is -0.5 \u2264 -0.4? Yes, it is. So the condition holds.\n\nNow take k=8, which is greater than 46/6\u22487.6667.\n\nCompute a=8\u00b2 -2*8 -15=64 -16 -15=33\n\nb=3*8 -7=24 -7=17\n\nc=2\n\nEquation:33x\u00b2 +17x +2=0\n\nCompute roots:\n\nD=17\u00b2 -4*33*2=289 -264=25\n\nsqrt(D)=5\n\nx=(-17 \u00b15)/(66)\n\nThus, x\u2081=(-17 -5)/66=-22/66=-1/3\u2248-0.3333\n\nx\u2082=(-17 +5)/66=-12/66=-2/11\u2248-0.1818\n\nCheck x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082: -1/3 \u2248-0.3333 \u22642*(-2/11)= -4/11\u2248-0.3636\n\nIs -0.3333 \u2264 -0.3636? No, because -0.3333 is greater than -0.3636. Therefore, the condition does not hold for k=8. Which aligns with our previous result that k must be \u226446/6\u22487.6667.\n\nAnother test with k=46/6\u22487.6667:\n\nk=46/6=23/3\u22487.6667\n\nCompute a=(23/3)^2 -2*(23/3) -15\n\nFirst, (23/3)^2=529/9\u224858.7778\n\n2*(23/3)=46/3\u224815.3333\n\nSo, a=529/9 -46/3 -15=529/9 -138/9 -135/9=(529 -138 -135)/9=(529 -273)/9=256/9\u224828.4444\n\nb=3*(23/3) -7=23 -7=16\n\nc=2\n\nEquation: (256/9)x\u00b2 +16x +2=0\n\nMultiply through by 9 to eliminate denominators:\n\n256x\u00b2 +144x +18=0\n\nDiscriminant D=144\u00b2 -4*256*18=20736 -18432=2304=48\u00b2\n\nRoots:\n\nx=(-144 \u00b148)/(2*256)= (-144 \u00b148)/512\n\nx\u2081=(-144 -48)/512= -192/512= -3/8\u2248-0.375\n\nx\u2082=(-144 +48)/512= -96/512= -3/16\u2248-0.1875\n\nCheck x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082: -3/8 \u2248-0.375 \u22642*(-3/16)= -3/8\u2248-0.375. So, equality holds here. Therefore, at k=23/3\u22487.6667, the condition is exactly satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the critical value is k=23/3\u22487.6667, and for k \u226423/3, the condition holds, and for k >23/3, it doesn't.\n\nTherefore, the interval where the condition is satisfied is [6,23/3], and the length is 23/3 -6=23/3 -18/3=5/3\u22481.6667.\n\nThus, the probability is (5/3)/5=1/3\u22480.3333.\n\nBut let's verify another k in [6,23/3]. Take k=7.5=15/2.\n\nCompute a=(15/2)^2 -2*(15/2) -15=225/4 -15 -15=225/4 -30=225/4 -120/4=105/4=26.25\n\nb=3*(15/2) -7=45/2 -14/2=31/2=15.5\n\nc=2\n\nEquation:26.25x\u00b2 +15.5x +2=0\n\nCompute discriminant D=(15.5)^2 -4*26.25*2=240.25 -210=30.25=5.5\u00b2\n\nRoots:\n\nx=(-15.5 \u00b15.5)/(2*26.25)= (-15.5 \u00b15.5)/52.5\n\nx\u2081=(-15.5 -5.5)/52.5= -21/52.5= -0.4\n\nx\u2082=(-15.5 +5.5)/52.5= -10/52.5\u2248-0.1905\n\nCheck x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082: -0.4 \u22642*(-0.1905)= -0.381. Is -0.4 \u2264 -0.381? Yes, because -0.4 is less than -0.381.\n\nThus, condition holds for k=7.5.\n\nNow, take k=23/3\u22487.6667, which we already did, and it holds as equality.\n\nTake k=7.8, which is above 23/3\u22487.6667.\n\nk=7.8=39/5\n\nCompute a=(39/5)^2 -2*(39/5) -15=1521/25 -78/5 -15=1521/25 -390/25 -375/25=(1521 -390 -375)/25=756/25=30.24\n\nb=3*(39/5) -7=117/5 -35/5=82/5=16.4\n\nc=2\n\nEquation:30.24x\u00b2 +16.4x +2=0\n\nDiscriminant D=16.4\u00b2 -4*30.24*2=268.96 -241.92=27.04=5.2\u00b2\n\nRoots:\n\nx=(-16.4 \u00b15.2)/(2*30.24)= (-16.4 \u00b15.2)/60.48\n\nx\u2081=(-16.4 -5.2)/60.48\u2248-21.6/60.48\u2248-0.357\n\nx\u2082=(-16.4 +5.2)/60.48\u2248-11.2/60.48\u2248-0.185\n\nCheck x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082: -0.357 \u22642*(-0.185)= -0.37. Is -0.357 \u2264 -0.37? No, because -0.357 is greater than -0.37. Therefore, condition fails.\n\nSo, this aligns with our previous conclusion.\n\nTherefore, it seems that the condition x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082 is satisfied when k \u226423/3\u22487.6667, and not satisfied otherwise. Therefore, the interval of k where the condition holds is [6,23/3], which has length 23/3 -6=5/3. Hence, the probability is (5/3)/5=1/3.\n\nTherefore, the probability is 1/3.\n\nBut wait, just to make sure, let's recap the steps to ensure there were no mistakes.\n\n1. We started by noting that k is in [6,11], and the quadratic equation must have real roots. We computed the discriminant D=(k-13)^2, which is always non-negative, so real roots for all k.\n\n2. Since a=k\u00b2-2k-15=(k-5)(k+3). For k in [6,11], a is positive, so parabola opens upwards, hence x\u2081 \u2264x\u2082.\n\n3. The condition x\u2081 \u22642x\u2082 translates to an inequality in terms of k. By expressing x\u2081 and x\u2082 via quadratic formula and manipulating the inequality, we arrived at k \u226423/3\u22487.6667.\n\n4. Verified with examples that for k \u226423/3, the condition holds, and for k >23/3, it doesn't.\n\n5. Calculated the length of the interval [6,23/3] as 5/3, divided by total length 5, giving probability 1/3.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 1/3, or in boxed form: \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{3}}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the probability that the roots of the quadratic equation \\((k^2 - 2k - 15)x^2 + (3k - 7)x + 2 = 0\\) satisfy \\(x_1 \\leq 2x_2\\) when \\(k\\) is randomly chosen from the interval \\([6, 11]\\), we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Discriminant Analysis**:\n   - The discriminant of the quadratic equation is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     D = (3k - 7)^2 - 4(k^2 - 2k - 15) \\cdot 2 = (k - 13)^2\n     \\]\n   - Since the discriminant \\(D = (k - 13)^2\\) is always non-negative, the quadratic equation has real roots for all \\(k\\).\n\n2. **Leading Coefficient Sign**:\n   - The leading coefficient \\(a = k^2 - 2k - 15\\) factors to \\((k - 5)(k + 3)\\). For \\(k \\in [6, 11]\\), \\(a\\) is positive, so the parabola opens upwards.\n\n3. **Condition \\(x_1 \\leq 2x_2\\)**:\n   - For the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) with roots \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) (where \\(x_1 \\leq x_2\\)), the condition \\(x_1 \\leq 2x_2\\) translates to:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{-b - \\sqrt{D}}{2a} \\leq 2 \\cdot \\frac{-b + \\sqrt{D}}{2a}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying this inequality leads to \\(k \\leq \\frac{23}{3}\\).\n\n4. **Interval Calculation**:\n   - The interval \\([6, 11]\\) where \\(k\\) is chosen has a length of 5.\n   - The interval where \\(k \\leq \\frac{23}{3}\\) (approximately 7.6667) within \\([6, 11]\\) is \\([6, \\frac{23}{3}]\\), which has a length of \\(\\frac{5}{3}\\).\n\n5. **Probability Calculation**:\n   - The probability is the ratio of the length of the interval where the condition holds to the total interval length:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\frac{5}{3}}{5} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the probability that the roots of the equation satisfy \\(x_1 \\leq 2x_2\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\n1. **Using Vieta's Theorems**: By Vieta's formulas, the sum and product of the roots \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) of the quadratic equation \\((k^2 - 2k - 15)x^2 + (3k - 7)x + 2 = 0\\) are given by\n\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   \\begin{array}{l}\n      x_1 + x_2 = \\frac{7 - 3k}{k^2 - 2k - 15} \\\\\n      x_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\frac{2}{k^2 - 2k - 15} \n   \\end{array}\n   \\right.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Condition Setup**: Determine the value of \\(k\\) such that \\(x_1 = 2x_2\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   \\begin{array}{l}\n      x_1 = 2x_2 \\\\\n      3x_2 = \\frac{7 - 3k}{k^2 - 2k - 15} \\\\\n      2x_2^2 = \\frac{2}{k^2 - 2k - 15}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\right.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solution for \\(x_2\\)**:\n\n   \\[\n   x_2 = \\frac{7 - 3k}{3(k^2 - 2k - 15)}\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   x_2^2 = \\frac{1}{k^2 - 2k - 15}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finding \\(k\\)**: Substitute the expressions for \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_2^2\\) into the second equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(7 - 3k)^2}{9(k^2 - 2k - 15