ex angle \u2220BAD = 180\u00b0 - 2\u03b3.\n\nNow, in triangle ABT, we have angle at B as \u03b1, and in triangle ADT, angle at D as \u03b2. Since T is on AC, the line BT and DT are connecting B and D to T. Maybe considering the triangles ABT and ADT could lead us somewhere. Since AB = AD, if we can show that BT = DT or something similar, that might help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the Law of Sines in triangles ABT and ADT. Let's try that.\n\nIn triangle ABT:\nAB / sin(\u2220ATB) = BT / sin(\u2220BAT) = AT / sin(\u03b1)\n\nIn triangle ADT:\nAD / sin(\u2220ATD) = DT / sin(\u2220DAT) = AT / sin(\u03b2)\n\nBut AB = AD, so we can write:\n\nAB / sin(\u2220ATB) = BT / sin(\u2220BAT) = AT / sin(\u03b1)\nAB / sin(\u2220ATD) = DT / sin(\u2220DAT) = AT / sin(\u03b2)\n\nHmm, since \u2220BAT and \u2220DAT are angles at A in triangles ABT and ADT. Since T is on AC, the line AC splits angle \u2220BAD into two parts: \u2220BAC and \u2220CAD. Wait, but since AB = AD, maybe there's some relation here.\n\nWait, let's think about point T. If we can find some relationship between BT and DT, perhaps using the angle condition \u03b1 + \u03b2 = \u2220BCD.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use some cyclic quadrilateral properties. If the sum of angles at T from B and D is related to angle at C, perhaps there is a cyclic quadrilateral involved here. Let me check.\n\nIf points B, T, D, and C lie on a circle, then \u2220BTD would be equal to \u2220BCD, but the given condition is \u03b1 + \u03b2 = \u2220BCD. Not sure if that directly relates. Alternatively, maybe if we can show that BT and DT make certain angles that would imply cyclic quadrilateral.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use trigonometric identities to express the given angle condition. Let me consider the coordinates. Maybe coordinate geometry could help here.\n\nLet me set up a coordinate system. Let\u2019s place point A at the origin (0,0). Since AB = AD, let's let AB and AD lie along the x-axis and y-axis respectively, making triangle AB