n\nSet the two equations equal:\n\n(-k/h)x - k\u00b2/h = (-h/k)x + h\n\nMultiply both sides by hk to eliminate denominators:\n\n(-k^2)x - k^3 = -h^2 x + h^2 k\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n(-k^2 x + h^2 x) + (-k^3 - h^2 k) = 0\n\nFactor x:\n\nx(-k^2 + h^2) - k(k^2 + h^2) = 0\n\nTherefore:\n\nx = [k(k^2 + h^2)] / (h^2 - k^2)\n\nSince the triangle is acute, all angles are less than 90 degrees. Therefore, the apex angle at B is less than 90 degrees, which in terms of coordinates would imply h > k, perhaps? Wait, in triangle ABC, angle at B is between AB and BC. Since AB and BC are equal, and if h > k, then the triangle is tall, making the apex angle smaller. Wait, maybe not exactly. Let's think about the coordinates.\n\nIn the coordinate system, point B is at (0, h), and points A and C are at (-k, 0) and (k, 0). The length AB is sqrt(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2), and the base AC is 2k. For the triangle to be acute, all angles must be less than 90 degrees. The angle at B is determined by the sides AB, BC, and AC. Using the Law of Cosines:\n\nAC\u00b2 = AB\u00b2 + BC\u00b2 - 2 AB * BC * cos(angle at B)\n\nBut AB = BC = sqrt(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2), and AC = 2k. So:\n\n(2k)^2 = 2(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2) - 2(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)cos(angle at B)\n\nSimplify:\n\n4k\u00b2 = 2(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)(1 - cos(angle at B))\n\nThen,\n\n1 - cos(angle at B) = (4k\u00b2) / [2(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)] = 2k\u00b2 / (k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)\n\nThus, cos(angle at B) = 1 - 2k\u00b2/(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2) = (k\u00b2 + h\u00b2 - 2k\u00b2)/(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2) = (h\u00b2 - k\u00b2)/(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)\n\nSince the angle at B is acute, cos(angle at B) > 0, so h\u00b2 - k\u00b2 > 0, meaning h > k.\n\nSo in our coordinate system, h > k. Therefore, the denominator h\u00b2 - k\u00b2 in the expression for x-coordinate of D is positive.\n\nSo x-coordinate of D is [k(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)] / (h\u00b2 - k\u00b2)\n\nThen, substitute x into the equation of BC to find y-coordinate:\n\ny = (-h/k)x + h = (-h/k)[k(k\u00b2 + h\u00b2)/(h\u00b2 -