\nTherefore, c\u00b2 - a c = ( r^4 / a\u00b2 ) - a ( r\u00b2 / a ) = r^4 / a\u00b2 - r\u00b2 = r\u00b2 ( r\u00b2 / a\u00b2 - 1 ) = - r\u00b2 ( 1 - r\u00b2 / a\u00b2 ) = - b\u00b2\n\nTherefore, c\u00b2 - a c = - b\u00b2\n\nThus, c\u00b2 - a c + t\u00b2 b\u00b2 = - b\u00b2 + t\u00b2 b\u00b2 = b\u00b2 ( t\u00b2 - 1 )\n\nThus, the difference is 2 ( b\u00b2 ( t\u00b2 - 1 ) )\n\nTherefore, unless t\u00b2 = 1, which corresponds to X being B or C, the difference is non-zero. But the problem states that for any X on BC, KX = XL. Therefore, there must be an error in my approach.\n\nThis suggests that either my coordinate setup is incorrect, or my computations are flawed. Alternatively, maybe the problem has additional constraints not mentioned.\n\nWait, let me verify the problem statement again: From point A, tangents AB and AC are drawn to a circle with center O. Through point X on segment BC, a line KL is drawn perpendicular to XO (points K and L lie on lines AB and AC). Prove that KX = XL.\n\nWait, in the problem statement, KL is drawn through X and perpendicular to XO. In my coordinate setup, I assumed KL is perpendicular to XO at X, which matches the problem statement. But according to my calculations, KX \u2260 XL except when X is midpoint. This suggests either a miscalculation or a misinterpretation.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"a line KL is drawn perpendicular to XO\". It doesn't specify that it's perpendicular at X. Wait, hold on! Wait, the problem says \"a line KL is drawn perpendicular to XO\". So, KL is a line perpendicular to XO, passing through X. Therefore, KL is the line through X perpendicular to XO. Therefore, it is perpendicular at X, so my initial assumption was correct. Therefore, my calculations must be wrong.\n\nBut according to the problem statement, it should hold for any X on BC. However, according to my calculations, it's only true when t = 0, which is the midpoint. This is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, I must have made a mistake in my coordinat