ll intersections are either above line n or below line m? Let me pick points C and D on line n but closer to AB. Let's choose C at (1,1) and D at (1,1). Wait, same point. No, need two different points.\n\nAlternatively, pick C at (a,1) and D at (b,1), such that lines AC and BD intersect between m and n.\n\nFor line AC from (0,0) to (a,1), the equation is y = (1/a)x.\n\nFor line BD from (2,0) to (b,1), the equation is y = [1/(b - 2)](x - 2).\n\nSetting them equal: (1/a)x = [1/(b - 2)](x - 2)\n\nMultiply both sides by a(b - 2):\n\n(b - 2)x = a(x - 2)\n\nbx - 2x = ax - 2a\n\nbx - ax = 2x - 2a\n\nx(b - a - 2) = -2a\n\nSo x = (-2a)/(b - a - 2)\n\nTo have x between 0 and 2 (assuming AB is from 0 to 2), we need the denominator and numerator to have appropriate signs.\n\nThis is getting too algebraic. Maybe there's a geometric insight I'm missing.\n\nWait, going back to the original problem: two parallel lines and a segment on one of them. Need to find midpoint with ruler.\n\nPerhaps a simpler method. Since the lines are parallel, if we can construct a triangle on the other line that allows us to apply the intercept theorem.\n\nHere's an idea:\n\n1. Let\u2019s mark two points C and D on line n.\n2. Connect A to C and B to D, forming lines AC and BD.\n3. Let these lines intersect at point E.\n4. Connect A to D and B to C, forming lines AD and BC.\n5. Let these lines intersect at point F.\n6. Draw line EF; this line should intersect AB at its midpoint.\n\nThis is similar to the construction of the midline in a complete quadrilateral. Let me verify this with coordinates.\n\nLet\u2019s set coordinates: line m is y=0, AB is from (0,0) to (2,0). Line n is y=1. Let\u2019s choose points C(1,1) and D(3,1) on line n.\n\n- Line AC: connects (0,0) to (1,1). Equation: y = x.\n- Line BD: connects (2,0) to (3,1). Equation: y = x - 2.\n- Intersection E: Solve x = x - 2 \u2192 0 = -2. No solution; lines are parallel. Not helpful. So points C and D can't be spaced like that.\n\nLet me choose