ase Analysis**: Setting two variables to \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\) and the third to 0.\n   - Example: \\( x = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), \\( y = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), \\( z = 0 \\).\n   - Calculation: \\( \\cos \\frac{2\\pi}{3} + \\cos \\frac{2\\pi}{3} + \\cos 0 = -0.5 - 0.5 + 1 = 0 \\).\n   - Conclusion: The minimum value is \\( 0 \\).\n\n### Critical Points and Lagrangian Analysis\n- **Equal Variables**: \\( x = y = z = \\frac{4\\pi}{9} \\) gives a sum of \\( 3 \\cos \\frac{4\\pi}{9} \\approx 0.5208 \\), which is higher than 0.5.\n- **Mixed Variables**: Two variables at \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\) and one at \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\) gives a sum of 0.5, which is higher than 0.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe maximum value is achieved when two variables are 0 and the third is \\( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\). The minimum value is achieved when two variables are \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\) and the third is 0.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Maximum value: } \\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{2}}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Minimum value: } \\boxed{0}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the function \\( f(x, y, z) = \\cos x + \\cos y + \\cos z \\) and the constraint on the variable sum \\( x + y + z = \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\), where \\(x, y, z\\) are non-negative real numbers, we aim to find the maximum and minimum values of \\( f \\).\n\nWithout loss of generality, assume \\( x \\le y \\le z \\). Thus, we have:\n\n\\[ \\frac{4\\pi}{9} \\le z \\le \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\]\n\nLet \\( z = \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\), where \\( 0 \\le k \\le \\frac{8\\pi}{9} \\). Then the constraint becomes:\n\\[ x + y = k \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( z \\) into the function, we get:\n\\[ f(x, y, z) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) + \\cos x + \\cos y \\]\n\nGiven \\( x + y = k \\), the function simplifies to:\n\\[ f(x, y, z) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) + \\cos x + \\cos (k - x) \\]\n\nNow, we use the trigonometric identity for the sum of cosines:\n\\[ \\cos x + \\cos y = 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{x+y}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{x-y}{2} \\right) \\]\n\nPlugging in \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) with the constraint \\( x + y = k \\):\n\\[ \\cos x + \\cos (k - x) = 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2x - k}{2} \\right) \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ f(x, y, z) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) + 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2x - k}{2} \\right) \\]\n\n### Maximum Value:\nTo maximize \\( f(x, y, z) \\):\n- Maximize \\( 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2x - k}{2} \\right) \\):\n  - \\( \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\ge 0 \\): Maximized by taking \\( \\cos \\left( \\frac{2x - k}{2} \\right) = 1 \\)\n  - This implies \\( 2x - k = 0 \\Rightarrow x = y = \\frac{k}{2} \\)\n\n- Therefore:\n\\[ f = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) + 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) + 2 \\]\n\nTo maximize this expression, set \\( k = 0 \\):\n\\[ f = \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) + 2 \\]\n\\[ \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\\[ f = -\\frac{1}{2} + 2 = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nThus, the maximum value is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}} \\]\n\n### Minimum Value:\nTo minimize \\( f(x, y, z) \\):\n- Minimize \\( 2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2x - k}{2} \\right) \\):\n  - Take \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( y = k \\)\n  - This gives \\( \\cos(0) + \\cos(k) + \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) \\)\n\nSimplified:\n\\[ f = \\cos(0) + \\cos(k) + \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - k \\right) = 1 + \\cos(k) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos(k) + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin(k) \\]\n\nSet \\( k = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\):\n\\[ \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) \\]\n\\[ \\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nSo:\n\\[ f = 1 - \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "sta