f the pentagon as \\(a\\).\n\n4. Considering the areas of the triangles and using the symmetry:\n\n\\[ S_{\\triangle BCDE} = 5 \\times S_{\\triangle OCD} \\]\n\\[ \\because S_{\\triangle OCD} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times a \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ S_{\\triangle BCDE} = 5 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times a = \\frac{5}{2} a \\]\n\n5. The total area of the pentagon can also be calculated by summing up the areas of the triangles formed by the vertex \\(A\\):\n\n\\[ S_{ABCDE} = S_{\\triangle AED} + S_{\\triangle ADC} + S_{\\triangle ACB} \\]\n\nSince each triangle has an area:\n\\[ S_{\\triangle AER} = \\frac{1}{2} a \\cdot A R \\]\n\\[ S_{\\triangle AEP} = \\frac{1}{2} a \\cdot A P \\]\n\\[ S_{\\triangle AEQ} = \\frac{1}{2} a \\cdot A Q \\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[ S_{ABCDE} = \\frac{1}{2} a \\left( A R + A P + A Q \\right) \\]\n\n6. Equate the two expressions for the area of the pentagon:\n\n\\[ \\frac{5}{2} a = \\frac{1}{2} a \\left( A R + A P + A Q \\right) \\]\n\nSolving for \\(A R + A P + A Q\\):\n\\[ \\frac{5}{2} a = \\frac{1}{2} a \\left( A R + A P + A Q \\right) \\]\n\\[ 5 = A R + A P + A Q \\]\n\n7. Given \\(O P = 1\\), and using the symmetry in the problem, observe that:\n\\[ A O = A P - O P \\]\n\\[ A O = A P - 1 \\]\n\nThus, substituting and finding the sum:\n\\[ A R + A O + A Q =  A R + (A P - 1) + A Q = (A R + A P + A Q) - 1 \\]\n\\[ A R + A O + A Q = 5 - 1 \\]\n\\[ A R + A O + A Q = 4 \\]\n\nConclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{C}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In Gumiland, only natural numbers smaller than ten thousand are recognized. They flexibly address the representation of these numbers. The basic rule for describing \"guminumbers\" is that each number is written in the smallest possible base system, ensuring that the written number has at most four digits. Unfortunately, the numbers written in this way are not always uniquely decodable; for example, the four-digit guminumber 1101 simultane