\\\n   \\text{For base 4:} & \\quad 81 < x < 256 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $81$ to $255$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 5:} & \\quad 256 < x < 625 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $256$ to $624$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 6:} & \\quad 625 < x < 1296 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $625$ to $1295$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 7:} & \\quad 1296 < x < 2401 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $1296$ to $2400$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 8:} & \\quad 2401 < x < 4096 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $2401$ to $4095$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 9:} & \\quad 4096 < x < 6561 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $4096$ to $6560$)}, \\\\\n   \\text{For base 10:} & \\quad 6561 < x < 10000 \\quad \\text{(valid numbers are from $6561$ to $9999$)}.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n4. We are interested in three-digit numbers. In base $n$, a three-digit number ranges from $n^2$ to $n^3 - 1$. We need to check these ranges for given intervals:\n\n   - A number in the $n$-base system must be greater than $(n-1)^4 - 1$ to be valid as a \"gumisz\u00e1m\" for that base.\n   - This means we need the three-digit range $n^2$ to $n^3-1$ to fit within the valid \"gumisz\u00e1m\" ranges calculated above.\n\n5. Begin checking:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   \\text{Base 2:} & \\quad 4 \\leq x \\leq 7, \\\\\n   \\text{Base 3:} & \\quad 9 \\leq x \\leq 26.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n6. Numbers within these ranges are:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   \\text{Base 2:} & \\quad 4_{10} = 100_2, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 5_{10} = 101_2, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 6_{10} = 110_2, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 7_{10} = 111_2; \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   \\text{Base 3:} & \\quad 9_{10} = 100_3, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 10_{10} = 101_3, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 12_{10} = 110_3, \\\\\n                    & \\quad 13_{10} = 111_3.\n   \\]\n   \\]\n\n7. Comparing these, we find that the numbers expressed in binary (base 2) as $100_2, 101_2, 110_2, 111_2$ do not overlap with those expressed in ternary (base 3) within the range $16$ to $26$.\n\n8. The exercise demonstrates that no three-digit \"gumisz\u00e1m\" in base 2 or base 3 has more than one natural number representation.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Therefore, there is no three-digit number that represents multiple values in different bases.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On the base AC of an isosceles triangle ABC, point E is taken, and on the sides AB and BC, points K and M are taken such that KE is parallel to BC and EM is parallel to AB. What fraction of the area of triangle ABC does the area of triangle KEM occupy if BM:ME = 2:3?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this geometry problem. So, we have an isosceles triangle ABC with base AC. Point E is on AC, and then points K and M are on AB and BC respectively, such that KE is parallel to BC and EM is parallel to AB. We need to find the fraction of the area of triangle KEM compared to the original triangle ABC, given that the ratio BM to ME is 2:3. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the triangle. Since ABC is isosceles with base AC, that means AB and BC are the equal sides. Wait, no, actually, if the base is AC, then the two equal sides would be AB and BC? Wait, no, in a triangle, the base is usually considered the side that's not equal. Wait, maybe I need to clarify. In an isosceles triangle, the base is the side that is unique, and the other two sides are equal. So if ABC is isosceles with base AC, then sides AB and CB are equal? Wait, no, if the base is AC, then the two equal sides would be BA and BC? Wait, maybe the notation is confusing me. Let me draw it mentally.\n\nLet me assume that triangle ABC has base AC, so vertex B is the apex. Therefore, AB and BC are the legs, but in an isosceles triangle, the two legs are equal. Wait, no, if the base is AC, then AB and BC would