efore, length BM = sqrt( (e/2)^2 + ( - h e / c )^2 ) = sqrt( e\u00b2/4 + h\u00b2 e\u00b2 / c\u00b2 ) = e * sqrt( 1/4 + h\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, coordinates of E: (e, 0)\n\nCoordinates of M: ( (c + e)/2, h*(1 - e/c ) )\n\nTherefore, vector ME is E - M = ( e - (c + e)/2, 0 - h*(1 - e/c ) ) = ( (2e - c - e)/2, - h*(1 - e/c ) ) = ( (e - c)/2, - h + h e / c )\n\nSo length ME = sqrt( ( (e - c)/2 )\u00b2 + ( - h + h e / c )\u00b2 )\n\n= sqrt( ( (c - e)/2 )\u00b2 + ( h ( e / c - 1 ) )\u00b2 )\n\n= sqrt( ( (c - e)\u00b2 ) / 4 + h\u00b2 ( (e - c)/c )\u00b2 )\n\n= sqrt( (c - e)\u00b2 / 4 + h\u00b2 (c - e)\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\n= (c - e) * sqrt( 1/4 + h\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, BM = e * sqrt(1/4 + h\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nME = (c - e) * sqrt(1/4 + h\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, BM / ME = e / (c - e ) = 2 / 3\n\nSo e / (c - e ) = 2 / 3 => 3e = 2c - 2e => 5e = 2c => e = (2/5)c\n\nTherefore, e = (2/5)c\n\nTherefore, the position of E is at (2c/5, 0 )\n\nNow, with e = 2c/5, we can find coordinates of K and M.\n\nEarlier, we found that:\n\nPoint K is (e/2, (e h)/c ) = ( (2c/5)/2, (2c/5 * h)/c ) = ( c/5, (2h)/5 )\n\nPoint M is ( (c + e)/2, h*(1 - e/c ) ) = ( (c + 2c/5 )/2, h*(1 - 2/5 ) ) = ( (7c/5 )/2, h*(3/5 ) ) = ( 7c/10, 3h/5 )\n\nNow, we need to find the area of triangle KEM. The coordinates of the three points are:\n\n- K: (c/5, 2h/5 )\n\n- E: (2c/5, 0 )\n\n- M: (7c/10, 3h/5 )\n\nTo compute the area of triangle KEM, we can use the shoelace formula.\n\nLet me list the coordinates:\n\nK: (c/5, 2h/5 )\n\nE: (2c/5, 0 )\n\nM: (7c/10, 3h/5 )\n\nArrange the points in order (K, E, M) and apply the formula:\n\nArea = 1/2 | x1(y2 - y3) + x2(y3 - y1) + x3(y1 - y2) |\n\nPlugging in:\n\nx1 = c/5, y1 = 2h/5\n\nx2 = 2c/5, y2 = 0\n\nx3 = 7c/10, y3 = 3h/5\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: x1(y2 - y3) = (c/5)(0 - 3h/5) = (c/5)( -3h/5 ) = -3ch/25\n\nSecond term: x2(y3 - y1) = (2c/5)(3h/5 - 2h/5) = (2c/5)(h/5) = 2ch/25\n\nThird term: x3(y1 - y2) = (7c/10)(2h/5 - 0) = (7c/10)(2h/5